problem_statement:
SatInstance::to_dimacs_path slow (unbuffered)
Some benchmarks on macOS (using a 23MB uncompressed CNF file):

This took ≈ 30 seconds:
```
instance.to_dimacs_path(filename)?;
```

This also took ≈ 30 seconds:
```
let mut f = File::create(filename)?;
instance.to_dimacs(&mut f)?;
```

But this was only ≈ 0.2 seconds:
```
let f = File::create(filename)?;
let mut f = BufWriter::new(f);
instance.to_dimacs(&mut f)?;
```

It looks like `to_dimacs_path` should wrap things in `BufWriter`; now it is apparently doing just `File::create` + `to_dimacs`, which can be very slow.
rustsat_minisat::simp segmentation fault
Sample code:

```
use rustsat::instances::SatInstance;
use rustsat::solvers::Solve;
use std::error::Error;
use std::result::Result;

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
    let instance: SatInstance = SatInstance::from_dimacs_path("example.txt")?;
    let mut solver = rustsat_minisat::simp::Minisat::default();
    solver.add_cnf(instance.as_cnf().0)?;
    println!("solving...");
    let res = solver.solve()?;
    println!("{res}");
    Ok(())
}
```

Input file: [example.txt](https://github.com/chrjabs/rustsat/files/14796073/example.txt)

This seems to crash every time with a "Segmentation fault" right after it prints "solving...". Tried these systems, with the same result:
- Intel iMac with macOS 12.7.4
- Apple M3 MacBook Pro with macOS 14.4
- some random Intel x86 Linux 

It works fine if I change `rustsat_minisat::simp` to e.g. `rustsat_minisat::core` (or any other solver).

Versions:
- Rust 1.77.0
- rustsat 0.4.3
- rustsat-minisat 0.2.4

